      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop a correctness-first exact replacement for spanning-tree enumeration: enumerate simple paths directly using adjacency lists, a visited set, and incremental color counts. Prove pruning is safe, handle disconnected/singleton inputs, and validate both path and output-format acceptance before timing.",
        "B": "Research a single-thread, low-latency branch-and-bound variant of direct path search. Focus on compact graph/color storage, eliminating vector/map copies and repeated color recomputation, plus safe reachable-color upper bounds and early stop at K. It must retain an exhaustive fallback and be measured against A and baseline.",
        "C": "Evaluate adaptive four-core search only after establishing a fast serial kernel. Split independent top-level path branches with private state and deterministic reduction; avoid parallel setup for small searches. Investigate structural preprocessing that safely shrinks components/search space. Reject it if overhead or synchronization loses on the full suite.",
        "G": "Integrate only complete, independently correct candidates. Preserve the established input/output behavior; do not assume a different optimal path is accepted without validation. Benchmark whole programs repeatedly on every supplied case versus baseline and candidates, choose the fastest valid program (or baseline), and do not blindly merge optimizations since gains are non-additive.",
        "analysis": "Baseline is only 7.76 ms, so latency and validation matter more than speculative threading. The source repeatedly builds dense matrices, enumerates spanning trees, and copies/searches vectors/maps while rediscovering paths; direct exact simple-path search is the main hypothesis. The 4-core target makes parallelism a conditional experiment, not a presumed win."
